              "title": "Gift Economies",
              "paragraphs": [
                "Long before currency systems emerged, human societies developed complex networks of reciprocal exchange that anthropologists now call gift economies. These systems operated on fundamentally different principles than modern markets, with social relationships and communal obligations taking precedence over individual profit. The practice of gift-giving created bonds of mutual dependence that structured entire communities.",
                "The potlatch ceremonies of Pacific Northwest indigenous peoples provide a striking illustration of gift economy principles in action. During these elaborate gatherings, hosts distributed blankets, copper goods, and food to guests in displays that demonstrated wealth through generosity rather than accumulation. Recipients incurred social debts that they would repay at future ceremonies, creating ongoing cycles of obligation. These cycles reinforced alliances between families and villages across generations and maintained social hierarchies through the strategic timing and scale of gifts.",
                "However, anthropologist have questioned whether gift economies truly operated outside economic calculation. Marcel Mauss argued that gifts always carried implicit expectations of return, making them a form of interested exchange disguised as generosity. Some scholars suggest that gift-giving served strategic purposes, such as establishing dominance or creating dependence. Modern research indicates that reciprocity norms vary considerably across cultures, challenging universal models of how these systems functioned."
              ],
