              "audio": "media/audio/set4/l2-q08-11.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "So today, let's look at one of the most fundamental concepts in economics: supply and demand. Understanding this will help you make sense of why prices change and how markets work.\nLet's start with demand. Demand refers to how much of a product consumers want to buy at different prices. Generally, when prices are low, people want to buy more. When prices are high, people buy less. This makes intuitive sense, right? If your favorite snack suddenly costs twice as much, you'd probably buy it less often.\nSupply is the opposite side. It refers to how much producers are willing to sell at different prices. When prices are high, producers want to make and sell more because they'll earn more profit. When prices are low, they produce less because it's not as profitable.\nNow, here's where it gets interesting. In a market, supply and demand interact to determine the actual price. The equilibrium price is where the amount consumers want to buy equals the amount producers want to sell. If the price is too high, there's a surplus because supply exceeds demand. If it's too low, there's a shortage because demand exceeds supply. The market naturally pushes toward equilibrium.\nThis basic model helps explain everything from why concert tickets sell out to why gas prices fluctuate. Next, we will examine factors that cause supply and demand curves to shift."
