              "audio": "media/audio/set4/l2-q12-15.mp3",
              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "Today we're going to explore symbiosis. Now, symbiosis basically refers to close, long-term relationships between different species. These relationships are everywhere in nature, and they come in several forms. Let me walk you through the main types.\nFirst, there's mutualism. This is when both species benefit from the relationship. A classic example is bees and flowers. Bees get nectar for food, and flowers get pollinated so they can reproduce. Both sides gain something valuable. You also see mutualism with certain bacteria in our digestive system—they help us break down food, and we provide them with a place to live.\nThe second type is parasitism. In this relationship, one organism benefits while the other is harmed. Think of ticks or mosquitoes. They feed on blood from their host, gaining nutrition, but the host loses blood and may even get sick. Parasites don't usually kill their hosts right away, though, because that would end their food source.\nFinally, we have commensalism. Here, one species benefits, and the other is neither helped nor harmed. Small fish that attach to sharks are a good example. The fish get free transportation and access to food scraps, while the shark isn't really affected either way.\nUnderstanding these relationships helps us see how interconnected ecosystems really are. When one species is affected, it can have ripple effects throughout the entire community of organisms."
